    "faq_trout": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_soggy": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I stop the bread going soggy?",
            "text": "<p>Two things do the work. <strong>Hollow the loaf</strong> so the wet ingredients sit down inside the crumb rather than pressing against the crust, and <strong>spread the soft cheese on both cut faces</strong> &mdash; it is a fat barrier between the bread and everything juicy. Then keep the greens between the tomato layer and the top half. Built that way it will hold for a couple of hours.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_scapes": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What are garlic scapes, and what if I cannot find them?",
            "text": "<p>The flower stalks garlic sends up in early summer &mdash; mild, green and faintly garlicky, with a season of about three weeks at farmers' markets. <strong>Chives are the direct swap</strong> and are in every shop year round. Spring onion tops work too. Do not substitute raw garlic; it is far too aggressive against smoked fish.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make it ahead or pack it for later?",
            "text": "<p>It is designed to travel. Build it whole, wrap it tightly in parchment or foil and leave it unsliced &mdash; the pressure of the wrap actually helps it set. A couple of hours at cool temperature is fine, and slicing just before you eat keeps the cut faces from drying. Overnight is too long; the cucumber starts weeping.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_cheese": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Bovre, chevre or cream cheese?",
            "text": "<p>Any of them. Bovre and chevre are tangier and cut the smoke a little better; cream cheese is milder and closer to a classic lox bagel. Whichever you use, it needs to be genuinely soft and spreadable when it goes on, otherwise it tears the crumb of the hollowed loaf.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_mortar": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I need a mortar and pestle for the basil?",
            "text": "<p>No, but do not reach for a blender. A mortar bruises the leaves and releases the oils while leaving texture; a blender turns it into a smooth, slightly bitter purée. Without a mortar, pile the basil up, chop it roughly with a sharp knife and stir the oil, lemon and salt through it. Same result.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_defrost": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I defrost Seatopia smoked trout?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in its sealed pack. It is already hot-smoked and ready to eat, so there is no cooking step afterwards &mdash; just open it and layer it. In a hurry, the sealed pack in a bowl of cold water for 20&ndash;30 minutes works. Never thaw at room temperature or in the microwave.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_soggy",
        "q_scapes",
        "q_ahead",
